AWS Get Code connectivity diagnosis — 2026-11-28

Scope: read-only AWS inspection, strict-host-key SSH reads, TLS probes, and
sanitized service logs. RDS and EC2 were started with the user's authorization.
No Security Group, Elastic IP association, or machine trust-store changes were
made. No SMTP email or OTP was sent.

AWS state after startup:
- RDS: available.
- EC2: running; instance and system health checks passed.
- systemd dantophong: active and enabled.
- EC2 listeners: TCP 9000 present; UDP 9001 absent.
- No 0.0.0.0/0 or ::/0 ingress was found for TCP 9000, UDP 9001, or TCP 1433.
- The AWS Free Tier API returned no public-IPv4 usage record; cost eligibility
  for a new Elastic IP remains unverified.

Root cause of the Launcher connection failure:
- The packaged client endpoint DNS resolves to an Elastic IP whose network
  interface is identified by AWS as an RDS network interface, not the target
  EC2 instance. TCP 9000 at that configured endpoint timed out.
- The target EC2 has a different, automatically assigned public DNS name. Its
  running TLS certificate matches the configured SHA-256 pin and the old
  configured DNS/IP SAN, but does not match the current EC2 DNS SAN. The
  fail-closed Launcher correctly rejects that hostname mismatch.
- The service journal contains the previously reported unexpected EOF. No
  current OTP attempt occurred because the packaged endpoint cannot reach the
  EC2 listener.
- The packaged Launcher displayed `Cannot connect to server: Connection timed
  out or was cancelled.` during startup. This verified the more useful
  connection diagnostic, not Get Code or SMTP delivery.

SMTP isolation:
- SMTP host is smtp.gmail.com on port 465. SMTP user, password, and sender
  values are configured; values are intentionally withheld.
- Outbound TLS handshake to smtp.gmail.com:465 succeeded.
- No SMTP AUTH or message-delivery result was claimed; an OTP was not sent.

Required resolution:
- Do not move the existing Elastic IP: it is associated with an RDS network
  interface, and reassigning it could disrupt database connectivity.
- A stable endpoint requires a new Elastic IP for EC2 (potential ongoing
  public-IPv4 charges) or an explicitly approved alternative. A temporary
  current-EC2-DNS solution requires certificate rotation and a refreshed pin,
  and that DNS may change after a stop/start.

## Follow-up after explicit approval — 2026-11-28

- An EIP dedicated to EC2 was associated after user approval. The existing
  RDS-associated EIP was not moved, and no Security Group rules were changed.
- The EC2 certificate was rotated for its current DNS SAN. Live TLS hostname
  and SHA-256 pin verification passed. A first rotation attempt rolled back;
  the script was corrected to wait for TCP readiness before committing.
- The current Linux Release Server was published and deployed to the existing
  service path. A rollback copy of the prior app directory was retained.
  systemd is active; the existing RDS reports `available`; the Server journal
  confirms database connection; TCP 9000 and UDP 9001 listeners are present.
- The friend ZIP was rebuilt with the verified TLS endpoint and pin. The
  Launcher executable, Unity player, TLS-enabled configuration, and archive
  checksum were verified locally (SHA-256:
  `A09C6C4C807B414AF2032B153B84C7166B6A650B639B932CA9FB772650F3E142`).
- One live registration-code request, submitted before the Server Release
  deployment, was accepted over the verified TLS connection. The user
  confirmed receipt of the email. No recipient address, username, OTP, SMTP
  credential, or connection string is recorded here; no duplicate email was
  sent after deployment.
- Local protocol/OTP-queue smoke passed after the update. GUI code entry,
  wrong/expired-code behavior in the Launcher, two-network multiplayer, and
  Internet gameplay remain unverified. AWS remains running for the user's
  final test; no RDS creation/redeployment or AWS stop was performed.
